        "Action: Keep the benefits of formal education while building a parallel system of self-directed learning and real-world projects.\n3.4 Information sources and authority\nMost people cannot personally verify every fact they rely on. Modern knowledge is specialised, and people must often depend on experts, researchers, journalists, institutions, and other intermediaries.",
        "That dependence is not inherently irrational. The challenge is deciding when trust is justified and how much confidence a claim deserves.",
        "The source raises concerns about:\n- Corporate ownership of media.\n- Advertising incentives.\n- Reliance on elite sources.\n- Search ranking systems.\n- Academic funding.\n- Professional gatekeeping.\n- The way information circulates between institutions.\nThese are reasons to examine incentives and methods, not reasons to dismiss all journalism, science, universities, or expert knowledge.",
        "Use a source-evaluation checklist:\n1. Claim: What exactly is being asserted?\n2. Evidence: What data, documents, or observations support it?\n3. Method: How was the evidence collected?\n4. Source: Who produced it, and what expertise do they have?\n5. Incentives: Who funds or benefits from the work?\n6. Independence: Have other credible sources reached similar conclusions?\n7. Uncertainty: What limitations or alternative explanations are acknowledged?\n8. Correction: Does the source correct errors transparently?\n3.5 Social media and algorithmic influence\nSocial platforms do more than distribute information. Their recommendation systems influence what people repeatedly see.",
        "The source describes social media as an amplifier of mimetic desire: people begin wanting things partly because they see other people appearing to want or possess them.",
        "This can happen through:\n- Lifestyle highlights.\n- Status symbols.\n- Popular opinions.\n- Beauty and fitness ideals.\n- Career and wealth narratives.\n- Viral consumption trends.\n- Public displays of relationships and achievement.\nRepeated exposure can make an externally generated desire feel personal.",
        "Before pursuing a new goal or purchase, ask:\n- Did I want this before I saw it repeatedly online?\n- Do I want the actual experience or the status associated with it?\n- Would I still choose it if nobody could see it?\n- Does it support a goal I deliberately selected?\n- What is the opportunity cost?\n3.6 AI as an amplifier of existing patterns\nAI can help people learn, reason, create, and solve problems. It can also encourage intellectual passivity if users outsource every question, accept fluent answers without checking them, or allow generated language to replace their own thinking.",
        "The source refers to a 2024 Max Planck Institute finding concerning changes in human speech patterns associated with ChatGPT use. The transcript does not provide the study title or enough detail to assess its methods, so the claim should be checked against the original research before being relied upon.",
        "The broader concern is worth considering: if people repeatedly consume and reproduce the same machine-generated patterns, their expression may become more standardised.",
        "A practical way to use AI without surrendering your thinking:\n- Form your own initial opinion before asking AI.\n- Ask it to challenge your reasoning, not merely agree.\n- Request counterarguments and alternative explanations.\n- Verify important factual claims using reliable sources.\n- Use it to explain difficult material, then restate the idea in your own words.\n- Attempt problems yourself before asking for a solution.\n- Keep your own notes, observations, and creative work.\n- Avoid entering private or sensitive information unless the tool and context are appropriate.\nPrinciple: Use AI as a thinking partner and tool—not as a substitute for having thoughts.\nChapter 3 audit: Your influence map\nCreate a table with the following columns:\n| Influence | Belief or habit I may have absorbed | Useful part | Possible limitation | What I will investigate |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Family |  |  |  |  |\n| School or university |  |  |  |  |\n| Friends and peers |  |  |  |  |\n| News and media |  |  |  |  |\n| Social platforms |  |  |  |  |\n| AI tools |  |  |  |  |\n| Workplace or profession |  |  |  |  |",
