        "Part II — Build Your Own Direction",
        "4.1 Replace inherited goals with self-generated goals\nThe source argues that people often pursue a default sequence: study, get a job, earn money, and retire. That sequence can work for some people, but it should not be accepted as the only possible definition of success.",
        "A self-generated goal begins with your own considered priorities.",
        "It should answer:\n- What do I want to become capable of?\n- What kind of problems do I want to solve?\n- What experiences matter to me?\n- What contribution would feel meaningful?\n- What kind of daily life am I trying to create?\n- What trade-offs am I willing to accept?\nA goal does not need to be unconventional to be authentic. You may examine the conventional path and still choose parts of it.\n4.2 Create an anti-vision\nAn anti-vision is a description of the future you do not want to drift into.",
        "It is not meant to make you hate your current life. It is a contrast that helps reveal what you need to change.",
        "Write a concrete description of a future you want to avoid:\n- What does an ordinary day look like?\n- What are you spending your time on?\n- Which skills have you failed to develop?\n- Which relationships have you neglected?\n- What financial position are you in?\n- What opportunities have you avoided?\n- Which decisions have you repeatedly postponed?\nBe specific. “I do not want to be unsuccessful” is too vague to guide action.",
        "For example:\nI do not want to spend years consuming information about building things while never completing a project. I do not want my decisions to be driven entirely by other people's approval. I do not want to reach a point where I have no useful skills, no financial options, and no confidence that I can learn something difficult.",
        "Your anti-vision should reflect your own concerns, not someone else's idea of a bad life.\n4.3 Turn the anti-vision into a positive vision\nAn anti-vision shows what you want to avoid. A positive vision describes what you want to build instead.",
        "Include:\n- Capabilities you want to develop.\n- Work you want to do.\n- Relationships you want to maintain.\n- Financial conditions you want to improve.\n- Problems you want to solve.\n- Experiences you want to have.\n- Standards you want to live by.\nThen convert the vision into goals that can guide decisions.",
        "A useful goal has:\n1. Direction: What are you moving toward?\n2. Evidence: How will you know you are progressing?\n3. Challenge: What capability must you develop?\n4. Next action: What can you do this week?\n5. Review: When will you evaluate the result?\n4.4 Understand the hidden goal behind inaction\nThe source makes an important observation: people may claim to want one thing while their behaviour reveals that another goal is taking priority.",
        "For example:\n- You say you want to build a business, but you prioritise avoiding embarrassment.\n- You say you want to get fit, but you prioritise immediate comfort.\n- You say you want to learn, but you prioritise entertainment.\n- You say you want independence, but you prioritise approval.\nThis does not mean you are lazy or dishonest. It means your actual incentives and fears may be stronger than your stated intention.",
        "Ask:\n- What do I gain by staying the same?\n- What discomfort am I avoiding?\n- What would change if I acted?\n- What am I afraid other people might think?\n- What small action would test whether this fear is justified?\n4.5 Sit with uncertainty instead of outsourcing your direction\nWhen people do not know what they want, they may immediately search for a guru, framework, career list, or personality test to provide the answer.",
        "External resources can help, but they cannot make the decision on your behalf.",
        "Use a period of deliberate reflection:\n1. Write down the options you are considering.\n2. Identify what attracts you to each option.\n3. Separate your own interest from social approval.\n4. List the costs and trade-offs.\n5. Run a small experiment where possible.\n6. Review what you learned.\n7. Choose the next step rather than waiting for perfect certainty.\nChapter 4 worksheet: Vision and anti-vision\nMy anti-vision\n- A life I do not want to drift into:\n- Habits that could lead me there:\n- Decisions I keep postponing:\n- Discomfort I need to become willing to face:\nMy positive vision\n- The capabilities I want to develop:\n- The problems I want to solve:\n- The kind of work I want to do:\n- The relationships and experiences I want to protect:\n- The next measurable milestone:\n- One action I will take this week:"
