        "Part III — Develop High Agency",
        "6.1 What agency means\nThe source defines agency as the ability to act without waiting for permission.",
        "A high-agency person does not assume that every difficult task is impossible simply because they cannot do it immediately. They investigate what is required, acquire the relevant skills and resources, and take action.",
        "Agency does not mean ignoring rules, refusing help, or assuming you can do everything alone. It means taking responsibility for identifying the next useful action rather than waiting passively for someone else to solve the problem.\n6.2 Separate easy, difficult, and impossible goals\nThe source divides goals into three categories:\n| Goal type | Meaning | Response |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Easy | You can do it with your current skills and resources | Execute |\n| Difficult | You cannot do it yet, but can potentially learn or acquire what is needed | Break it down and build capability |\n| Impossible | It cannot be achieved under the relevant constraints or laws of reality | Reconsider the goal or its assumptions |",
        "A common mistake is treating difficult goals as impossible because the path is unfamiliar.",
        "Suppose you want to build a software product but have never written code. You cannot build the complete product today. That does not prove the goal is impossible. You can learn fundamentals, build a small prototype, seek feedback, and improve.",
        "The right question is:",
        "“What would I need to learn, practise, or acquire to make this possible?”\n6.3 Replace an external locus of control with responsibility\nThe source criticises an external locus of control: the tendency to attribute outcomes mainly to luck, other people, or circumstances.",
        "Some outcomes genuinely depend on factors outside your control. A realistic sense of agency does not deny those constraints.",
        "Instead, distinguish:\n- What you control.\n- What you can influence.\n- What you cannot control.\nThen direct your effort toward the first two categories.",
        "For example:\n| Situation | Outside your control | Within your influence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Job search | The total number of vacancies | Your skills, applications, portfolio, and interview preparation |\n| Business | Market conditions and competitor decisions | Customer research, offer quality, delivery, and outreach |\n| Learning | How quickly every concept becomes clear | Study consistency, practice, and asking better questions |\n| Creative work | Whether every post becomes popular | Quality, originality, frequency, and feedback |",
        "This prevents both helplessness and unrealistic self-blame.\n6.4 Practise solving problems before asking for rescue\nThe source recommends delaying the impulse to ask others for help so that you practise finding solutions yourself.",
        "Use this carefully. The goal is not to refuse support when it is needed or to waste hours struggling alone.",
        "Try this sequence:\n1. State the problem precisely.\n2. Identify what you already know.\n3. Search for relevant information.\n4. Attempt a small solution.\n5. Record what failed.\n6. Ask a specific question if you remain stuck.\n7. Apply the answer and explain it back in your own words.\nThis builds problem-solving ability while still allowing collaboration.\n6.5 Take on difficult projects regularly\nThe source recommends consciously exposing yourself to difficult problems every three to six months.",
        "A project should stretch your current ability while remaining possible with sustained effort.",
        "Examples:\n- Build and publish a small software project.\n- Create a portfolio around a skill.\n- Learn a new technical discipline.\n- Organise a community event.\n- Research and write a substantial guide.\n- Develop a product prototype.\n- Complete a challenging physical or creative goal appropriate to your circumstances.\nThe point is to develop confidence through evidence. Each completed project shows that you can learn, adapt, and work through uncertainty.\n6.6 Question the default next step\nAgency is not merely doing more. It also involves deciding whether the action you are expected to take is worth taking.",
        "Before following a default path, ask:\n- Why is this the expected next step?\n- What outcome is it meant to produce?\n- Is it the best route for my goal?\n- What alternatives exist?\n- What would I need to learn before choosing differently?\n- What is the cost of delaying the decision?\nYou may still choose the conventional option. The difference is that it becomes a decision rather than an automatic response.\n6.7 Do not make drastic education decisions casually\nThe source includes a provocative suggestion to leave school or request homeschooling as an exercise in agency. That is not a universal recommendation and should not be followed without careful consideration.",
        "Education decisions can affect qualifications, finances, family circumstances, immigration or licensing requirements, and future opportunities.",
        "A safer application of the underlying principle is:\n- Take responsibility for your own learning while enrolled.\n- Build projects outside formal coursework.\n- Investigate alternative education routes before making a change.\n- Understand the consequences of leaving a programme.\n- Speak with relevant advisers or trusted people.\n- Make the decision based on your goals and circumstances, not as a symbolic act of rebellion.\nHigh agency means making an informed decision, not choosing the most extreme option.\nChapter 6 exercise: The difficult-goal breakdown\nChoose one difficult goal.\n- What do I want to accomplish?\n- Why does it matter?\n- What makes it difficult right now?\n- Which skills am I missing?\n- Which resources do I need?\n- What is the smallest first step?\n- What can I complete in seven days?\n- Who or what can provide useful feedback?\n- How will I measure progress?\nReview the plan every week and adjust it based on evidence.\nPart IV — Become a Creator"
