        "Part IV — Become a Creator",
        "7.1 Human progress comes from solving problems\nThe source frames humans as tool builders. People encounter problems—cold, hunger, danger, distance, inefficiency—and develop tools and systems that help solve them.",
        "The broader principle is that creation begins with a problem.",
        "A creator does not have to be an artist or a content creator. A creator can be someone who:\n- Builds software.\n- Designs a product.\n- Improves a process.\n- Teaches a useful skill.\n- Develops a service.\n- Writes a practical guide.\n- Organises a community.\n- Creates a tool that saves time.\n- Finds a better way to solve an existing problem.\nCreation is the act of turning knowledge, imagination, and effort into something useful.\n7.2 Connect purpose with contribution\nThe source links enjoyment to progress and contribution: people can find meaning in improving their capabilities while solving problems that matter beyond themselves.",
        "This gives you two questions:\n1. What kind of challenge would help me grow?\n2. Who could benefit from the result?\nA project becomes more meaningful when it combines personal interest with a real contribution.",
        "This does not mean every hobby must become a business. Some activities are valuable because they are enjoyable, restorative, or meaningful without producing money.\n7.3 Find the intersection of interest and value\nA useful creation opportunity often sits at the intersection of:\n- Interest: You are curious enough to stay engaged.\n- Capability: You can develop the skills required.\n- Problem: Someone has a real need.\n- Contribution: Your work improves an outcome.\n- Sustainability: You can continue the work without unacceptable costs.\nThe source also points to the intersection of purpose and profit: solving problems you find interesting and creating solutions that others value.",
        "A simple opportunity test:\n| Question | What you are looking for |\n| --- | --- |\n| Do I care about this problem? | Enough interest to keep learning |\n| Does the problem affect other people? | A real audience or user |\n| Is the current solution inadequate? | A reason to improve it |\n| Can I create a useful alternative? | A feasible first version |\n| Can people find and use it? | A distribution path |\n| Could it sustain itself? | A plausible economic model if commercial |",
        "7.4 Start where you can create and distribute\nThe source identifies the internet as a high-leverage place to create because people can publish work, share knowledge, and reach audiences without first receiving permission from a traditional gatekeeper.",
        "The internet provides routes to:\n- Publish writing.\n- Share educational material.\n- Release software.\n- Sell digital products.\n- Offer services.\n- Build communities.\n- Collect feedback.\n- Find collaborators and customers.\nBut publishing is not the same as creating value. A post, website, or product matters when it helps a particular person understand something, solve a problem, or achieve an outcome.\n7.5 Distribution is part of creation\nA useful solution has limited impact if the people who need it cannot find it.",
        "Think about distribution early:\n- Where does the intended audience already spend time?\n- What questions do they ask?\n- Which communities do they trust?\n- What format helps them understand the solution?\n- How can they try or access the work?\n- How will you learn from their response?\nThe source's final principle is to share the value you acquire in a place where the right people can find it.\n7.6 Build a creator's progression\nUse this sequence:\n1. Explore: Find a problem or subject that interests you.\n2. Learn: Acquire the knowledge and skills needed.\n3. Create: Build a small useful output.\n4. Publish: Put it where relevant people can access it.\n5. Observe: Collect feedback and watch how people use it.\n6. Improve: Correct weaknesses and add useful features.\n7. Repeat: Build a stronger body of work over time.\nDo not wait until you feel like an expert. Start with a small, honest contribution and improve."
