        "Part V — Make It Sustainable",
        "9.1 Work toward a self-generated goal\nThe source connects flow or optimal experience with three conditions:\n- A goal generated by the person.\n- Clarity about how to make progress.\n- A level of challenge slightly above current skill.\nThe practical implication is that vague ambition is difficult to act on. A goal should be clear enough to guide the next step, but challenging enough to require growth.\n9.2 Match challenge to capability\nIf a task is far below your ability, it may become boring. If it is far beyond your current ability, it may become overwhelming.",
        "Choose a task that stretches you while remaining approachable through learning and practice.",
        "For example, if you are learning web development:\n- Too easy: repeatedly copy a tutorial without understanding it.\n- Too difficult: attempt to build a complex platform with no foundation.\n- Better challenge: build a small feature that requires you to learn one new concept and apply it.\nIncrease complexity as your capability grows.\n9.3 Use feedback to maintain direction\nA goal becomes easier to pursue when you can see whether your actions are working.",
        "Create feedback loops:\n- A project has a working demonstration.\n- A learning plan has practical exercises.\n- A fitness goal has an appropriate progress measure.\n- A business experiment has customer feedback and cost tracking.\n- A writing goal has completed drafts and reader responses.\nReview progress regularly. If the method is not working, change the method rather than abandoning the goal immediately.\n9.4 Novelty and challenge can support learning\nThe source argues that novelty, challenge, and ambitious goals can influence neuroplasticity—the brain's capacity to change with experience.",
        "The practical lesson is to avoid remaining permanently inside familiar tasks. Periodically take on a project that requires you to learn something new.",
        "Do not interpret this as a reason to seek constant intensity or extreme experiences. Sustainable challenge is more useful than perpetual disruption."
